riscv: esp32p4: coherent DMA through the non-cacheable alias

dma_alloc_coherent() could not succeed on this platform, and the kernel said
so at every boot:

  coherent DMA allocations not supported on this platform.
  dw_mmc 50083000.mmc: IDMAC: cached descriptor ring with manual cache sync

NOMMU has no second mapping available to mark uncached, so dma_direct_alloc()
found neither ARCH_HAS_DMA_SET_UNCACHED nor DMA_DIRECT_REMAP nor a global
pool and returned NULL. Drivers then had two options, both bad: fail, or
open-code a cached descriptor ring with hand-written cache maintenance.
dw_mmc took the second (0007) and getting the maintenance subtly wrong is
what made the SD card unusable until 0036. stmmac cannot take it at all --
it allocates its rings in ndo_open and simply refuses to come up:

  stmmaceth 50098000.ethernet eth0: stmmac_setup_dma_desc: DMA descriptors
  allocation failed

There is a third option, and it was in the SoC all along. The ESP32-P4
aliases memory *behind* its cache at a fixed +0x40000000, for both PSRAM and
internal SRAM. ESP-IDF names the constants and says what they are:

  /* non-cacheable offset for memory behind the cache */
  #define SOC_NON_CACHEABLE_OFFSET_SRAM   0x40000000
  #define SOC_NON_CACHEABLE_OFFSET_PSRAM  0x40000000

So the platform can implement arch_dma_set_uncached() the way MIPS does with
UNCAC_BASE: allocate an ordinary page, hand the CPU the aliased address, hand
the device the real one. dma_direct_alloc() already calls
arch_dma_prep_coherent() first, which on riscv evicts dirty lines through the
cached alias, so the handover is clean.

VERIFIED ON HARDWARE

An assumption this load-bearing should not be taken from a header comment,
and it cannot be tested from Linux -- /dev/mem refuses anything past
high_memory, so reading 0x88000000 returns EFAULT from
valid_phys_addr_range() before it ever reaches the bus. The bootloader tests
it in M-mode instead (p4_psram_alias_test): write through the alias, read
back through the cached window, then write a *different* value through the
cached window and read the alias again with no cache maintenance between.

  p4boot: psram alias 0x88100000: cached-sees-alias 0x11111111,
          alias-sees-cached 0x11111111, alias-rw 0x33333333
  p4boot: psram alias: UNCACHED and coherent -- dma_alloc_coherent() can work

The middle number is the proof: the cached write of 0x22222222 was invisible
through the alias, so it was still sitting in the cache and the alias is
genuinely not going through it.

WHAT THIS REPLACES

An earlier attempt used DMA_GLOBAL_POOL with a shared-dma-pool in internal
SRAM, on the belief that internal SRAM is uncached on this part. It is not:
CACHE_LL_LEVEL_INT_MEM is 1 on the P4, meaning internal memory is cached by
L1, and esp_cache_get_line_size_by_addr() accordingly returns 64 for it
rather than 0. The pool came up correctly and then broke the SD card, with
0036's diagnostic firing intermittently after transfers had already worked --
the signature of a stale cache line. That patch is dropped; the two cannot
coexist anyway, since kernel/dma/Kconfig has
`depends on !ARCH_HAS_DMA_SET_UNCACHED` on DMA_GLOBAL_POOL.

ARCH_HAS_DMA_CLEAR_UNCACHED is deliberately NOT selected. The alias is an
address, not a mapping, so there is nothing to undo, and dma_direct_free()
recovers the page from the DMA address rather than from the CPU pointer.

Signed-off-by: f23 <ali@connectedmotion.io>
diff -urN a/arch/riscv/Kconfig.socs b/arch/riscv/Kconfig.socs
--- a/arch/riscv/Kconfig.socs	2026-09-10 19:39:52.517672859 +0300
+++ b/arch/riscv/Kconfig.socs	2026-09-10 19:39:52.853672974 +0300
@@ -109,6 +109,10 @@
 	select RISCV_DMA_NONCOHERENT
 	select RISCV_NONSTANDARD_CACHE_OPS
 	select NOMMU_USERSPACE_POOL
+	# This SoC aliases both PSRAM and internal SRAM uncached at
+	# +0x40000000, so dma_alloc_coherent() can return memory that really
+	# is coherent instead of failing. See arch/riscv/mm/dma-noncoherent.c.
+	select ARCH_HAS_DMA_SET_UNCACHED
 	help
 	  This enables support for the Espressif ESP32-P4 SoC.
 	  The ESP32-P4 features dual RV32IMAFC HP cores, LP core,
diff -urN a/arch/riscv/mm/dma-noncoherent.c b/arch/riscv/mm/dma-noncoherent.c
--- a/arch/riscv/mm/dma-noncoherent.c	2026-06-09 13:28:53.000000000 +0300
+++ b/arch/riscv/mm/dma-noncoherent.c	2026-09-10 19:39:52.853672974 +0300
@@ -155,3 +155,51 @@
 	if (!noncoherent_supported)
 		dma_cache_alignment = 1;
 }
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_SOC_ESP32P4
+/*
+ * The ESP32-P4 aliases memory behind its cache at a fixed +0x40000000, for
+ * both PSRAM and internal SRAM -- ESP-IDF calls the constants
+ * SOC_NON_CACHEABLE_OFFSET_PSRAM and SOC_NON_CACHEABLE_OFFSET_SRAM and
+ * comments them "non-cacheable offset for memory behind the cache".
+ *
+ * That is what makes dma_alloc_coherent() possible here. NOMMU has no second
+ * mapping to mark uncached, so without this dma_direct_alloc() gives up:
+ *
+ *   coherent DMA allocations not supported on this platform.
+ *
+ * and every driver with a descriptor ring either fails to open or open-codes
+ * a cached ring plus hand-written cache maintenance. That was the road that
+ * made the SD card unusable, and stmmac cannot take it at all.
+ *
+ * dma_direct_alloc() does the rest: it allocates an ordinary page, calls
+ * arch_dma_prep_coherent() to evict any dirty lines through the cached alias,
+ * hands the CPU the pointer this returns, and hands the device
+ * page_to_phys() -- the real address. The device is unaffected; only the
+ * CPU's route to the memory changes.
+ *
+ * VERIFIED ON HARDWARE, because an assumption this load-bearing should not be
+ * taken from a header comment. The bootloader writes a value through the
+ * alias, reads it back through the cached window, then writes a different
+ * value through the cached window and reads the alias again with no cache
+ * maintenance in between:
+ *
+ *   p4boot: psram alias 0x88100000: cached-sees-alias 0x11111111,
+ *           alias-sees-cached 0x11111111, alias-rw 0x33333333
+ *
+ * The middle number is the proof. The cached write of 0x22222222 was
+ * invisible through the alias, so it was still sitting in the cache and the
+ * alias is genuinely not going through it. See p4_psram_alias_test().
+ *
+ * No arch_dma_clear_uncached(): the alias is an address, not a mapping, so
+ * there is nothing to undo, and dma_direct_free() finds the page from the DMA
+ * address rather than from the CPU pointer. ARCH_HAS_DMA_CLEAR_UNCACHED is
+ * deliberately not selected.
+ */
+#define ESP32P4_NONCACHEABLE_OFFSET	0x40000000UL
+
+void *arch_dma_set_uncached(void *addr, size_t size)
+{
+	return (void *)((unsigned long)addr + ESP32P4_NONCACHEABLE_OFFSET);
+}
+#endif /* CONFIG_SOC_ESP32P4 */
